            "The <strong>research design</strong> is the overall plan for answering the question: who will be studied, whether they will be compared with anyone, whether the researcher will do anything to them or merely observe, when and how often they will be measured, and how the threats to a true answer will be controlled. There is no best design — only the design that fits the question, the resources and the ethics of the situation — and the verb in the question names it more reliably than the title does. ‘What proportion…’ and ‘what are the characteristics of…’ ask for a <strong>descriptive</strong> design; ‘is X associated with Y?’ asks for an <strong>analytical observational</strong> design — cross-sectional, cohort or case-control — in which the researcher observes exposures and outcomes as they occur without changing anything; ‘does X cause Y?’ or ‘what is the effect of X on Y?’ asks for an <strong>experimental</strong> design, in which the researcher herself decides who receives X and then compares the outcomes. Lesson 1.1 placed these on a hierarchy of evidence for questions of effect; this lesson walks up it, and lesson 2.2 turns to the qualitative designs that answer ‘why’ and ‘what is it like’.",
