            "The <strong>cross-sectional study</strong> measures exposure and outcome in the same people at the same moment — a single snapshot — and is the commonest analytical design in nursing research because it is quick, cheap and needs no follow-up. Its natural measure is <strong>prevalence</strong>, the proportion of the sample who have the condition at that moment, and it can compare prevalence between exposed and unexposed (‘hypertension is more prevalent among traders who sit all day than among those who walk the market’). Its weaknesses follow from its design: because exposure and outcome are measured together it cannot say which came first — did the back pain cause the nurse to move to the outpatient department, or did the department cause the pain? — so it shows association and not sequence; it over-represents long-lasting conditions and misses the short and the fatal (a snapshot of a ward finds the patients who stay longest); and it depends on the sample being representative, which lesson 3.1 takes up. Repeated cross-sectional surveys of a population — the Demographic and Health Surveys of 2003, 2008, 2014 and 2022 — show trends in the population even though they follow no individual.",
