            "A <strong>cohort study</strong> starts with the exposure. The researcher identifies a group of people free of the outcome, records who is exposed and who is not — nurses who work night shifts and nurses who do not; infants who were exclusively breastfed and infants who were not; women who received iron and folate in pregnancy and women who did not — and follows both groups forward in time to count who develops the outcome. Its measures are <strong>incidence</strong> — the rate at which new cases arise in each group, which lesson 5.2 defines — and the <strong>relative risk</strong>, the incidence in the exposed divided by the incidence in the unexposed, which lesson 6.2 interprets. Because exposure is recorded before the outcome appears, the cohort establishes <strong>temporal sequence</strong>, can study several outcomes of one exposure, and can study exposures that would be unethical to assign (nobody can be randomised to smoke, or to be delivered by an untrained attendant); against that, it is slow and expensive, loses people to follow-up — those lost are often different from those who remain, which biases the result — and is useless for rare outcomes, because a cohort of 2,000 women will contain too few maternal deaths to compare. A <strong>retrospective (historical) cohort</strong> uses records to reconstruct exposure in the past and outcome up to the present, gaining speed at the price of record quality; the Navrongo and Kintampo demographic surveillance sites, which have followed whole populations for decades, make Ghana one of the few African countries where large cohorts can be assembled from existing data.",
