            "Qualitative sampling is <strong>purposive</strong>: participants are chosen deliberately because they have the experience the study needs and can speak about it, not at random to represent a population. The researcher may seek <strong>maximum variation</strong> (mothers young and old, first-time and experienced, trading and farming, so that the full range of experience is heard), <strong>typical</strong> or <strong>extreme</strong> cases, <strong>key informants</strong> who know the setting from the inside, or <strong>snowball</strong> referrals from one participant to the next when the group is hidden — people who inject drugs, men who have sex with men, women who have had unsafe abortions. The sample size is not calculated in advance but reached when data <strong>saturate</strong>: when the last two or three interviews have produced no new idea, no new code, no new variation. The proposal states the expected range (‘approximately 12 to 15 mothers, or until saturation’) and the report states where saturation was judged and how. A small sample is not a weakness of a qualitative study; a sample that stopped before saturation, or that heard only the easy-to-reach, is.",
            "The <strong>in-depth interview</strong> is the commonest method. It is guided by a <strong>topic guide</strong> — a short list of the areas to be covered and a few opening questions, not a questionnaire to be read aloud — and it begins with a broad, open invitation (‘tell me about the time your baby was born and what happened with feeding’) and follows the participant, using <strong>probes</strong> (‘can you say more about that?’, ‘what happened next?’, ‘what did your mother-in-law say?’, a silence, a nod) to go deeper rather than wider. The questions are <strong>open</strong>, never leading (‘did you stop because of work?’ puts the answer in the participant's mouth; ‘what led you to stop?’ lets her give it), and asked in the language the participant thinks in — Twi, Ewe, Dagbani, Ga, Hausa — by an interviewer who speaks it, with the guide translated and back-translated so that the question means in Ewe what it meant in English. The interview is <strong>audio-recorded</strong> with consent (a phone will do) and <strong>transcribed verbatim</strong>, pauses and laughter included, within days while the memory is fresh; an hour of interview takes four to six hours to transcribe, and a translated transcript is checked by a second speaker. The setting is private, the time is the participant's, and the interviewer records <strong>field notes</strong> immediately afterwards — the setting, the mood, what was not said, her own reactions — because the recording captures the words and not the room.",
