          alt: "Getting an honest answer about herbal use — most patients use it, most do not tell you, and the question is what decides which. Questions that close the conversation: ‘You are not taking any of that herbal thing, are you?’; ‘Do you use traditional medicine?’ — many will say no and mean a different word; ‘Why would you take that instead of your tablets?’; ‘You must stop all of it immediately.’ Each one teaches the patient to stop telling you, and she still takes it, now without your knowledge. Questions that open it: ‘Many people use something from home as well. What have you been taking?’; ‘Anything you drink, chew, rub on, or take as an enema?’; ‘Could you bring the bottle or the packet next time?’; ‘Who advised it, and what did they say it was for?’ Normalise it first, then ask what, not whether; the bottle answers questions the patient cannot. Why people use it: it is nearby and the clinic is not; it can be paid for in instalments or in kind; the healer explains the illness in her own language; the family expects it and she is not deciding alone; the hospital treatment failed or felt disrespectful. None of these are cured by telling her she is wrong. Ask about the fare and the waiting time before you ask about the beliefs.",
          caption: "Interviewing technique in one picture. The closed, leading and judgemental question produces the socially desirable answer; the normalised, open question in the participant's own words produces the truth — and the researcher's topic guide must be built the second way.",
